 ['Which regime displaced Yuan rule in China in 1368?','the Ming dynasty',['the Abbasid caliphate','the Safavid dynasty'],'The Ming replaced Yuan rule in China; Mongol political power continued elsewhere.'],
 ['A fictional comparison pairs a destroyed city with a later protected caravan. What is the strongest conclusion?','Mongol expansion could cause destruction and later facilitate exchange',['conquest was uniformly peaceful','trade and imperial violence could never coexist'],'A balanced explanation distinguishes destructive conquest from later policies supporting trade.'],
 ['A fictional court hires specialists from several conquered regions. Which broader Mongol-era pattern does this illustrate?','movement of skilled people and knowledge across regions',['complete isolation of conquered societies','the end of all technological borrowing'],'Imperial connections moved specialists and skills, sometimes through forced relocation.'],
 ['How did Mongol-era contacts relate to medical and mathematical knowledge reaching western Europe?','they contributed to wider exchanges that also used other routes',['they made Mongols the inventors of all Greek medicine','they were the only contacts Europe ever had'],'Mongol links were part of wider exchanges; transmission through Islamic societies and other routes also mattered.'],
 ['Which claim best expresses continuity during Mongol expansion?','merchants continued using older Eurasian trading networks',['all trade began only after 1206','the Silk Roads immediately ceased to exist'],'Mongol power changed security and political control while drawing on preexisting networks.']
